choice BENCHMARK_TEST
    prompt "Benchmark test to build"
    default BENCHMARK_TEST_EVENT_TASK_SWITCH_PREEMPT
    help
      Exactly one benchmark test is linked into the image: every test
      provides its own main() and its own set of measurement threads,
      so they cannot be built together.

config BENCHMARK_TEST_EVENT_TASK_SWITCH_PREEMPT
    bool "Task switch via k_event, preempting waiter"
    help
      timing/task_switch/preempt/time_task_switch_event.c

      S_A measures the L_Task -> H_Task switch triggered by k_event_post()
      when a higher priority waiter is pending. S_B measures the bare
      k_event_post() cost with no matching waiter.

config BENCHMARK_TEST_EVENT_TASK_SWITCH_BLOCK
    bool "Task switch via k_event, blocking waiter"
    help
      timing/task_switch/block/time_task_switch_event.c

      Same S_A / S_B pair, but the waiter blocks instead of preempting, so
      the measurement also covers the wait prologue and the no-wait tail.

config BENCHMARK_TEST_TASK_SWITCH_CHANGE
    bool "Task switch via k_thread_priority_set()"
    help
      timing/task_switch/task_change/time_task_switch.c

      Same S_A / S_B pair, but the switch is triggered by yield. All tasks
      are same priority.

config BENCHMARK_TEST_TASK_SWITCH_PREEMPT
    bool "Task switch via k_thread_resume()"
    help
      timing/task_switch/task_preempt/time_task_switch.c

      Same S_A / S_B pair, but the switch is triggered by resume. The
      waiter is higher priority than the poster.

config BENCHMARK_TEST_SEM_TASK_SWITCH_PREEMPT
    bool "Task switch via k_sem, preempting waiter"
    help
      timing/task_switch/sem_preempt/time_task_switch_sem.c

      Same S_A / S_B pair as the k_event preempt test, but the switch is
      triggered by k_sem_give() while a higher priority thread pends in
      k_sem_take(). S_B gives with no waiter, or with a waiter that cannot
      preempt the giver.

config BENCHMARK_TEST_INTERRUPT_RESPONSE_TIME
    bool "Interrupt latency via a self-induced interrupt"
    help
      timing/interrupt_latency/time_interrupt_response.c

      S_A measures from the API that pends the interrupt to the first
      instruction the aperiodic task runs after the ISR released it:
      exception entry, _isr_wrapper dispatch, the release call, the
      PendSV switch pended by z_arm_exc_exit() and the return into the
      task. S_B measures the same pend API alone, with the line disabled
      in the NVIC so no exception is taken. S_A - S_B is the
      interrupt-to-task path.

      TEST_CASE_I releases with k_thread_resume() against a
      self-suspended task, TEST_CASE_II with k_sem_give() against a
      pending waiter, TEST_CASE_III with k_event_post() against a
      waiter in k_event_wait_safe(). The semaphore and the event use a
      release object of their own, so the ISR never walks a wait queue
      holding anything but the aperiodic task. TEST_CASE_IV drops the
      release and closes the window on the first instruction of the ISR
      body.

      TEST_CASE_V, VI, VII and VIII repeat I, II, III and IV with the
      handler placed in the vector table by IRQ_DIRECT_CONNECT()
      instead of the software ISR table, so the pairs I/V, II/VI,
      III/VII and IV/VIII differ only by the _isr_wrapper dispatch.

config BENCHMARK_TEST_IPC_MAILBOX_ASYNC
    bool "Message passing via k_mbox, asynchronous"
    help
      timing/ipc/mailbox/mailbox_async.c

      Three windows over k_mbox_async_put() -> k_mbox_get(), each swept
      over a message size of 4 to 1024 bytes, doubling at each of the
      nine steps, with one series per size.

      T1 sends to the sending thread itself: the window covers the put,
      the match against the thread's own message on the tx queue and the
      copy, with no context switch. T2 sends to a waiter of higher
      priority, which preempts the sender inside the put, so the window
      adds the switch and closes on the receiver's k_mbox_get(). T3 sends
      to a waiter of lower priority, which runs only once the sender
      blocks, so the window also carries the sender's block.

      T2 and T3 are released by a third thread, T_ArmUp / T_ArmDown,
      priority 4 - below both measured threads. It can only be scheduled
      once the sender is blocked on the baton and the receiver is
      pending in k_mbox_get(), so giving the baton from there guarantees
      the receiver is already waiting when the put arrives. Both windows
      therefore take the same mbox_message_match() path, and the pair
      differs by the priority relation alone rather than by which path
      the message took. It also keeps the receiver's loop prologue out
      of the window, since no window can open until the receiver pends.

      The asynchronous put is what makes T1 possible at all - a
      synchronous k_mbox_put() pends the sender until a receiver
      consumes, so a thread cannot send to itself - and it is used in T2
      and T3 as well to keep one send path across the three windows. It
      requires NUM_MBOX_ASYNC_MSGS > 0.

config BENCHMARK_TEST_IPC_FIFO
    bool "Message passing via k_fifo"
    help
      timing/ipc/fifo/fifo.c

      The same three windows and the same nine sweep points as the
      k_mbox test, over k_fifo_put() -> k_fifo_get().

      k_fifo passes a reference: the kernel links the caller's item into
      the queue through the word the item reserves at its front, and
      hands the receiver that pointer. Nothing is copied, by the kernel
      or by the test, so the window holds the transfer alone and the
      sweep is a control rather than a variable - the nine points are the
      same measurement, and any spread across them is noise, not a cost
      of message size.

      T2 and T3 both hit the direct handoff: the releaser thread
      (priority 4, below both measured threads) hands the sender its
      baton only once the receiver is pending in k_fifo_get(), so no
      list node is built in either. T1 has no second thread and is the
      only window that goes through the queue's list.

config BENCHMARK_TEST_IPC_LIFO
    bool "Message passing via k_lifo"
    help
      timing/ipc/lifo/lifo.c

      Identical to the k_fifo test except for the object, so the pair
      isolates what the insertion end costs: k_lifo prepends where
      k_fifo appends, and k_fifo_put() has to walk to the list tail
      first. Everything else - item layout, the three windows, the
      sweep - is the same.

config BENCHMARK_TEST_IPC_STACK
    bool "Message passing via k_stack"
    help
      timing/ipc/stack/stack.c

      The same three windows and sweep, over k_stack_push() ->
      k_stack_pop().

      k_stack passes one word by value, out of a fixed array sized at
      k_stack_init(); the test pushes the address of the item, so
      nothing is copied here either. Unlike k_fifo the item needs no
      reserved word and no list node is ever built, which is what the
      comparison against it isolates.

config BENCHMARK_TEST_IPC_MSGQ
    bool "Message passing via k_msgq"
    help
      timing/ipc/msgq/msgq.c

      The same three windows and sweep, over k_msgq_put() ->
      k_msgq_get(). The message size is fixed at k_msgq_init(), so the
      sweep uses one queue per size, all initialized before the
      measurement starts.

      k_msgq copies by value, and how often depends on whether a reader
      is pending. T2 and T3 are released by the releaser thread only
      once the receiver is pending, so the put copies straight into the
      reader's buffer - one copy. T1 has no second thread: the put
      copies into the ring buffer and the get copies back out of it -
      two copies. k_mbox has no ring and always copies once, so the pair
      separates the buffering from the transfer.

config BENCHMARK_TEST_IPC_PIPE_ASYNC
    bool "Message passing via k_pipe, buffered"
    help
      timing/ipc/pipe/pipe_async.c

      The same three windows and sweep, over k_pipe_write() ->
      k_pipe_read().

      k_pipe copies by value through a byte ring buffer, and splits the
      same way as k_msgq: with a reader already pending,
      copy_to_pending_readers() copies straight into the reader's stack
      buffer and unpends it without touching the ring - one copy - which
      is the path T2 and T3 take, since the releaser thread lets the
      write start only once the receiver pends in k_pipe_read(). T1 has
      no second thread, so the write copies into the ring and the read
      copies back out - two copies.

      Against k_msgq the object is byte-oriented rather than
      message-oriented: the transfer length is an argument to each call
      instead of being fixed at k_msgq_init(), so one pipe per window
      covers all nine sweep points where k_msgq needs one queue per
      size. The ring is MESSAGE_SIZE_MAX bytes and every sweep size
      divides it evenly, so the write head returns to an aligned offset
      each iteration and no message straddles the wrap - one memcpy per
      ring_buf_put() and ring_buf_get() at every point, rather than two
      at the sizes that would otherwise split.

config BENCHMARK_TEST_IPC_MAILBOX_SYNC
    bool "Message passing via k_mbox, synchronous"
    help
      timing/ipc/mailbox/mailbox_sync.c

      The k_mbox test reduced to the two cross-thread windows and driven
      by the synchronous k_mbox_put() instead of k_mbox_async_put(). Two
      threads and no releaser: the rendezvous does what the baton did.

      k_mbox_put() returns only once the receiver has disposed of the
      message, so each direction settles into one repeating path from
      the first iteration. T1 (send to a higher priority receiver): the
      receiver is already pending, the put matches it and pends the
      sender, and the receiver preempts. T2 (send to a lower priority
      receiver): the sender reaches the put first, pends on the tx
      queue, and the receiver's get matches it there. Neither needs a
      third thread to establish who waits for whom.

      The window opens on the sender in both directions and closes on
      whichever thread is running when the transaction completes, which
      the priority relation decides. mbox_message_dispose() readies the
      sender and reschedules from inside k_mbox_get(), after the memcpy:
      in T1 the sender is the lower thread and does not preempt, so the
      receiver closes; in T2 the sender is the higher thread and takes
      the CPU back there, so the put returns and the sender closes. Both
      windows therefore hold the whole transfer and nothing else, and
      the memcpy is inside both - k_mbox_put() only exchanges
      descriptors, the copy is always k_mbox_data_get() on the receiver.

      The two windows are fully separated: a sender and a receiver of
      their own, a mailbox of their own, and a phase of their own, with
      I_Task creating each pair and aborting it before the next starts.
      Nothing is shared between them but the message buffers, so the
      pair differs by the priority relation alone.

      The self-send window of the asynchronous test has no counterpart
      here - a synchronous put pends until a receiver consumes, so a
      thread cannot send to itself.

config BENCHMARK_TEST_IPC_PIPE_SYNC
    bool "Message passing via k_pipe, bufferless"
    help
      timing/ipc/pipe/pipe_sync.c

      The k_pipe test in the same two thread shape as the synchronous
      k_mbox test, with the pipe initialized with no ring buffer at all.
      With nowhere to deposit bytes, k_pipe_write() can only complete by
      copying into a reader that is already pending, and otherwise
      blocks on the space wait queue - so the transfer is a rendezvous
      and the releaser is again unnecessary.

      T1 (write to a higher priority reader) is the clean case:
      copy_to_pending_readers() copies and unpends the reader inside
      k_pipe_write(), the reschedule hands it the CPU, and it closes the
      window on the return from k_pipe_read(). The window holds the
      write, the copy and the switch.

      T2 (write to a lower priority reader) does not close as tightly,
      and the difference is a property of the API rather than of the
      test. k_pipe_write() returns once the bytes are handed over, not
      once the reader has consumed them, so it is not the full
      rendezvous that k_mbox_put() is: there is no point at which the
      writer is held until the reader has closed a window. The steady
      state is one repeating cycle - the writer blocks on the space
      queue, the reader's k_pipe_read() wakes it, the copy runs on the
      writer and the window closes there - so the series is repeatable,
      but it spans the reader's return from the previous transfer and
      its loop back into k_pipe_read() as well as the transfer itself.
      T2 is therefore an upper bound on the transfer, and is not
      comparable to the k_mbox T2 window; T1 is.

endchoice

source "Kconfig.zephyr"
rsource "packages/benchmark_hms/Kconfig"
